# Do not run dependency lifecycle scripts on install.
#
# This tree resolves to ~1400 packages, and `preinstall`/`postinstall` hooks are
# the mechanism npm supply-chain worms propagate through — they execute on
# `npm ci` before any of our own code runs, in a job that has a checkout and a
# network. Turning them off closes that path for this project's own install.
#
# It does NOT close it repo-wide, and it is worth being exact about the limit.
# The `deploy` job in .github/workflows/docs.yml runs a second npm install —
# cloudflare/wrangler-action shells out to `npm i wrangler@3.x` — and that one
# has no checkout, so this file is not on disk there at all. It is covered
# separately, by NPM_CONFIG_IGNORE_SCRIPTS on that step, because npm reads
# config from the environment as well as from a file. If you add another npm
# install anywhere, assume this file does not reach it until you check.
#
# Verified safe for this project rather than assumed: exactly two packages in
# package-lock.json declare an install script, and neither is load-bearing.
#
#   core-js   — a postinstall console banner asking for donations. Cosmetic.
#   fsevents  — darwin-only optional native binding for chokidar's file watcher,
#               used by `npm start` (dev). Never installs on the Linux CI runner,
#               and a production build does not watch files.
#
# Re-check with:
#   jq -r '.packages | to_entries[]
#          | select(.value.hasInstallScript == true) | .key' package-lock.json
#
# If a future dependency genuinely needs its install script, prefer replacing
# the dependency over deleting this line.
ignore-scripts=true

# Deliberately NOT setting `minimumReleaseAge` here. npm 11.9 does not recognise
# the key — it emits "Unknown project config ... will stop working in the next
# major version" — so today it buys nothing but warning noise. It would also be
# the wrong layer: CI installs with `npm ci`, which resolves nothing and reads
# the committed lockfile verbatim, so a release-age floor could never fire there.
#
# A cooldown on *entry* into this lockfile is the control that would actually
# bite, and it belongs in the dependency updater's config rather than here.
# There is no such updater in this repo yet; wiring one up is the next change.
